Certainly! Below is a demonstration of improper use of the Java API that can lead to vulnerabilities in web applications, specifically focusing on SQL Injection. This is followed by a corrected version of the code.

```java
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.DriverManager;
import java.sql.ResultSet;
import java.sql.Statement;

public class VulnerableExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String userInput = "1 OR 1=1"; // Simulating user input that could be malicious
        String query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = " + userInput; // vulnerable to SQL Injection

        try (Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mydb", "user", "password");
             Statement statement = connection.createStatement()) {

            // Execute the vulnerable query
            ResultSet resultSet = statement.executeQuery(query);

            while (resultSet.next()) {
                System.out.println("User: " + resultSet.getString("username"));
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

***
```java
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.DriverManager;
import java.sql.PreparedStatement;
import java.sql.ResultSet;

public class SecureExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String userInput = "1"; // Simulating user input

        // Use PreparedStatement to prevent SQL Injection
        String query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?";

        try (Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mydb", "user", "password");
             PreparedStatement preparedStatement = connection.prepareStatement(query)) {

            // Set the parameter to prevent SQL Injection
            preparedStatement.setString(1, userInput);

            // Execute the secure query
            ResultSet resultSet = preparedStatement.executeQuery();

            while (resultSet.next()) {
                System.out.println("User: " + resultSet.getString("username"));
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

In the first snippet, the code is vulnerable to SQL Injection because it directly concatenates user input into the SQL query. In the second snippet, the code uses a `PreparedStatement` to safely parameterize the SQL query, thus preventing SQL Injection vulnerabilities.

